The direct planted-partition prototype fails the paper’s hardness gate: after translating “each color is a star forest” into NAE constraints on every 3-edge path, unit-propagating DPLL finds a decomposition in only tens to hundreds of branches. I’m therefore not shipping that false Track A claim.

I’m switching to an honest Track B family grounded in Lemma 4. Its ordinary algorithm scans hundreds of tripartite graph blocks and solves the resulting exact GF(2) system; the short route reads only the zero- and unit-tag blocks, while the submitted object is still an executable symbolic two-galaxy partition that the checker expands and validates edge by edge.
